---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Methodology | dogbitesettlementcalc.com';
const description = 'How the dog bite settlement calculator works: pain multiplier by severity, liability adjustment, and provocation reduction.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Methodology &mdash; How We Estimate Dog Bite Settlements</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Economic damages</h2><p>Medical costs plus lost wages, entered directly by the user. These are the most concrete and well-documented components of any personal injury claim.</p>
  <h2>Pain and suffering (non-economic damages)</h2><p>Calculated as medical costs &times; a severity multiplier: minor (2&times;), moderate (3.5&times;), severe (5&times;), catastrophic (8&times;). Lost wages are multiplied by 1.5. These multipliers reflect published insurance industry and jury verdict research for dog bite cases.</p>
  <h2>Liability adjustment</h2><p>Strict liability states: no reduction (1.0&times;). One-bite rule / negligence states: 0.6&times; adjustment reflecting the additional proof burden and likelihood of partial defenses. Actual adjustment depends on the specific facts of prior knowledge.</p>
  <h2>Provocation reduction</h2><p>No provocation: no reduction. Minor provocation: 20% reduction reflecting comparative fault. Significant provocation: 70% reduction reflecting potential contributory negligence or complete bar in some states.</p>
  <h2>Range</h2><p>&plusmn;40% for standard cases; &plusmn;50% for provocation cases, reflecting the higher factual uncertainty.</p>
</BaseLayout>
